## Rules that are easy to get wrong

- **There is no build step.** The plugin runs `main.py` under BranchKit's
  managed CPython, with the SDK vendored into this directory (`branchkit/`).
- **Call the platform through the SDK's typed methods**, e.g.
  `await plugin.collection_append(name=..., payload=...)` for the method
  `collection.append`: the method name in snake_case, keyword arguments only.
  `reference/specs/plugin-api.md` lists every method's params; the SDK is
  vendored here, in `branchkit/methods_gen.py`.
- **`actions_gen.py` is generated** from `action_types` in `plugin.json`.
  Never edit it by hand. After changing `action_types`, regenerate it from this
  directory: `go run github.com/branchkit/branchkit-gen@latest --plugin .`
  (needs Go 1.24 or newer).
- **The plugin is sandboxed** and gets only what `plugin.json` declares:
  privileges under `requires`, the collections it reads under `consumes`,
  network hosts, files. An undeclared call is refused at run time, and the
  tests may not catch it, so declare first: each method in
  `reference/specs/plugin-api.md` names the privilege it needs.
- **Collections this plugin provides are named `plugin.{{.PluginID}}.<name>`.**
  Other names belong to other plugins or the platform.
- **The settings tab uses Datastar 1.0** (`data-on:click`, `data-init`).
  Older forms (`data-on-click`, `data-on-load`) appear in examples on the web
  and silently do nothing here.
- **Log with the SDK**: `await plugin.info("TAG", {...})` (also `debug`, `warn`, `error`).
  Never print to stdout: stdout carries the JSON-RPC messages. Do not log
  the user's own text (what they said, typed, or have on screen).
- **Tests live in `test_main.py`** and run the plugin under
  `branchkit-test-harness`, a real matcher and event bus without the app.
  Simulate a phrase, inject an event, read a HUD channel or a collection:
  see `how-to/debug/test-a-plugin.md`; the rest of the harness API is in
  `branchkit/harness.py`.

## When it fails

- `dev test` prints one line per check; `!` is a warning, not a failure.
  A failing or warning check prints `docs: <page>`, a path under
  `$D`: read that page first. `dev test --json` gives the same as JSON.
- Only if the user has installed the plugin and turned on Developer Access on
  its card in Settings: `branchkit-cli dev plog {{.PluginID}} --since 60s`
  shows what it logged in the running app.
